          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"zuccoli-lamore-di-loredana-0b61bc5e855a3829\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Filippo’s embedded “ti amo io” presents his romantic love for Loredana as an established fact while asking whether she reciprocates. It is therefore a strong truth-conditional avowal. The surrounding proposal to elope does not turn this particular clause into an undertaking, and the trembling voice and charged scene do not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The avowal is embedded in a negatively framed question rather than asserted in a standalone sentence, but the comparative “come ti amo io” still presents Filippo’s love as given.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Filippo directly addresses Loredana; the love avowal occurs as the comparative embedded clause in his question, “Allora non mi ami d’amore, come ti amo io?”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Filippo uses his avowed love to solicit reciprocal acknowledgement after proposing that Loredana flee with him. The question exerts emotional pressure by treating his own love as certain and construing her silence as possible nonreciprocation. His larger speech offers love and a shared life, but the highlighted clause itself primarily avows an emotional state. The narrator later explicitly says that Filippo loved Loredana, while also revealing the ethically complicated context of his existing mistress and his calculated justification of an elopement.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Allora non mi ami d’amore, come ti amo io?”\",\"supports\":\"The comparative clause presents Filippo’s romantic love as true while asking whether Loredana loves him in the same way, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Filippo says, “So che ti amo, sento che ti amo” and offers Loredana his love and his life.\",\"supports\":\"The explicit surrounding avowals confirm that the highlighted “ti amo io” refers to a claimed emotional state; the separate offer belongs to the broader proposal rather than requiring P for the highlighted clause.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Filippo is pale and his voice trembles before asking the question.\",\"supports\":\"The delivery is emotionally intense, but it supplies no independent evidence that the words function as a reflex-like expressive discharge, so E remains unsupported.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator later states, “amava Loredana; sentiva che le sarebbe stato fedele.”\",\"supports\":\"The narration corroborates the sincerity of Filippo’s avowed love, although sincerity is not required for the T classification.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the embedded avowal. The proposal, emotional delivery, and pressure for reciprocation are adequately treated as context and create no residual core-force dimension beyond T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
